Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to reproduce a
Python utility module for large-file line reading and chunked processing, with
the following specification.
Build a Python 3 script (Termux-compatible shebang:
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`) that begins with `from
__future__ import annotations` and uses only the standard library.
Required imports: argparse, heapq, json, mmap, os, shutil, sys, tempfile,
collections.Counter, datetime/timezone, pathlib.Path, and typing (Any, Callable,
Iterable, Optional, Sequence).
Define these module-level constants: - MMAP_THRESHOLD = 1_048_576 (bytes; files
larger than this are read via memory mapping) - DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE = 100_000
(lines per chunk) - DEFAULT_ENCODING = "utf-8" - DEFAULT_WORKERS = 8 Define
logging helpers: `info(msg)` prints `[INFO] {msg}` to stdout; `warn(msg)` and
`err(msg)` print `[WARN] {msg}` and `[ERROR] {msg}` to stderr.
Core function `read_lines(path: Path, encoding=DEFAULT_ENCODING,
skip_empty=False) -> list[str]`: - Raise `FileNotFoundError(path)` if the path
does not exist. - Check file size via stat.
If size > MMAP_THRESHOLD, open the file in binary mode and memory-map it
read-only; otherwise use a normal read path. - In the mmap path, iterate by
locating `b"\n"` with `mm.find` from a moving start offset; the final segment
without a trailing newline is taken up to end of map.
Decode each chunk with the given encoding, strip trailing `\r\n` only (not other
whitespace), and append it unless `skip_empty` is True and the stripped line is
empty. - Handle decoding failures explicitly (catch UnicodeDecodeError, report
via `warn` or `err`, and continue or skip as sensible), and document the chosen
policy in code comments only if idiomatic. - The non-mmap path should behave
identically in output semantics (same line-ending stripping and skip_empty
behavior).
Remaining functionality to implement (the source file continues beyond the read
helper, so design these consistently with the above): - A chunked processing
pipeline that splits a line sequence into batches of `DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE`
(overridable by CLI), possibly writing intermediate chunks to a
`tempfile`-created directory that is cleaned up with `shutil.rmtree` in a
`finally` block. - Parallel processing using a worker pool of `DEFAULT_WORKERS`
(overridable), applying a caller-supplied `Callable` to each chunk. -
Aggregation of results using `collections.Counter` for frequency statistics and
`heapq.nlargest` for top-N reporting. - Optional JSON output via `json.dumps`
with `ensure_ascii=False`, including a UTC timestamp generated with
`datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()`. - File-system outputs should be
written atomically where practical (write to a temp file, then `os.replace`). -
Expose a `main()` function that builds an `argparse.ArgumentParser` with options
for input path, output path, encoding, chunk size, worker count, skip-empty,
top-N, and JSON output; return an integer exit code and call `sys.exit(main())`
under `if __name__ == "__main__":`.
Error handling and edge cases the code must cover: - Missing input file: report
via `err` and exit nonzero, without a traceback. - Empty input file: produce
valid empty output rather than crashing. - Files with no trailing newline, and
files with CRLF line endings. - Files exactly at or near MMAP_THRESHOLD;
zero-byte files (mmap cannot map empty files, so the non-mmap path must be used
for them). - Invalid encoding bytes. - Invalid argument values (non-positive
chunk size or worker count) rejected with clear messages. - Keyboard interrupt
handled gracefully with a nonzero exit code.
Keep comment density and naming idiomatic and consistent with standard Python
style, use type hints throughout, and produce a single runnable file with no
external dependencies. ---
